message:
Integrate the new user.js into game.js and dedupe logic. (#14)

https://chat.openai.com/share/e250d511-2739-4533-82aa-141aa01e31ad

I need an updated game.test.js now that game.switchUser is not a method.
Also if there is new coverage to add please add it.

ChatGPT
You're correct, since the switchUser method is now within the User
class, we don't need to test it in the Game.test.js anymore. Instead,
we'll focus on the game-related methods.

Here's the updated Game.test.js:

```javascript
import 'jest-localstorage-mock';
import Game from './game.js';

let game;

beforeEach(() => {
    localStorage.clear();
    // set localStorage values
    localStorage.setItem('user', 'testUser');
    localStorage.setItem('testUser', JSON.stringify({ 'July 2023': '1' }));

    document.body.innerHTML = `
        <p id="user"></p>
        <select id="rikishi">
            <option value="1">Rikishi 1</option>
            <option value="2">Rikishi 2</option>
        </select>
        <button id="startPlayingButton">Start Playing</button>
        <button id="switchUserButton">Switch User</button>
        <input id="userSwitch" type="text">
        <input id="backfillContest" type="text">
        <input id="backfillRikishi" type="text">
        <button id="backfillResultsButton">Backfill Results</button>
        <div id="backfilledResults"></div>
        <p id="feedback"></p>
    `;
    game = new Game();
});

test('check if startPlaying is defined and returns expected value', () => {
    document.querySelector('#rikishi').value = '1';
    const result = game.startPlaying();
    expect(result).toBe("You selected: 1\nPrevious Picks: {\"July 2023\":\"1\"}");
});

test('check if backfillResults updates the results in UI', () => {
    document.querySelector('#backfillContest').value = 'May 2023';
    document.querySelector('#backfillRikishi').value = '2';
    game.backfillResults();
    let resultsElement = document.querySelector('#backfilledResults');
    expect(resultsElement.textContent).toContain('May 2023: 2');
});

test('check if provideFeedback updates the feedback in UI', () => {
    game.provideFeedback('Test feedback message');
    expect(document.querySelector('#feedback').textContent).toBe('Test feedback message');
});
```

In this updated test file, I've removed the test for switchUser since
it's no longer a method in the Game class. I've also added a new test
for the provideFeedback method, to ensure that the method updates the
feedback in the UI as expected.

This should cover all the public methods in the Game class. The
initialize method is typically not unit tested because it mainly deals
with setting up event listeners and initializing UI elements, which are
tested individually by the other tests.

User
caught SyntaxError: The requested module './game.js' does not provide an
export named 'Game' (at (index):34:18)

ChatGPT
I apologize for the confusion. In the provided script in index.html, the
Game class is being imported as a named import. However, in the game.js
file, Game is the default export, not a named export.

Here's how you should correct it:

Change the import statement in index.html from:

```html
<script type="module">
    import { Game } from './game.js';
    window.game = new Game();
    window.game.initialize();
</script>
```

to:

```html
<script type="module">
    import Game from './game.js';
    window.game = new Game();
    window.game.initialize();
</script>
```

Please replace the corresponding line in your index.html file with this
one. Now, the Game class should be imported properly as the default
export from the game.js file.

Add Pick to the object model. (#17)

https://chat.openai.com/share/478c9404-79d8-4632-b82a-bb9fbad9648a

I am building a JavaScript application for a sumo wrestling game. In
this game, players select a wrestler for each basho in a wave. I need to
build a 'Pick' object that represents a pick made by a player. It should
contain the wrestler's name and potentially other relevant details.

ChatGPT
Sure! I can help you with that. In JavaScript, you can define an object
using either object literals or constructor functions. Here's an example
of how you can create a 'Pick' object for your sumo wrestling game:

```javascript
// Using object literals
const pick = {
  wrestlerName: '',
  // You can add more properties relevant to the pick if needed
};

// Using a constructor function
function Pick(wrestlerName) {
  this.wrestlerName = wrestlerName;
  // You can add more properties relevant to the pick if needed
}

// Usage
const myPick = new Pick('Sumo Wrestler 1');
console.log(myPick.wrestlerName); // Output: Sumo Wrestler 1
```

In the above code, the Pick object represents a pick made by a player.
It has a wrestlerName property to store the name of the wrestler
selected by the player. You can add additional properties to the object
as needed, such as waveNumber or bashoNumber to keep track of the
specific wave or basho for which the pick is made.

You can create a new instance of the Pick object using either object
literals (the first example) or constructor functions (the second
example). In both cases, you can assign the wrestler's name to the
wrestlerName property.

User
I need a class and pick.test.js

ChatGPT
Certainly! Here's an example of how you can define a Pick class in
JavaScript along with a pick.test.js file for testing:

pick.js:

```javascript
class Pick {
  constructor(wrestlerName) {
    this.wrestlerName = wrestlerName;
    // You can add more properties relevant to the pick if needed
  }
}

module.exports = Pick;
```

pick.test.js:

```javascript
const Pick = require('./pick');

// Test case 1
const myPick = new Pick('Sumo Wrestler 1');
console.log(myPick.wrestlerName); // Output: Sumo Wrestler 1

// Test case 2
const anotherPick = new Pick('Sumo Wrestler 2');
console.log(anotherPick.wrestlerName); // Output: Sumo Wrestler 2
```

In the above code, the Pick class is defined with a constructor that
takes the wrestler's name as a parameter. You can add more properties to
the class as needed.

The module.exports = Pick; statement in pick.js exports the Pick class,
making it available for import in other files.

The pick.test.js file demonstrates a couple of test cases. It imports
the Pick class using require('./pick') and creates instances of the Pick
class. The console.log statements are used to verify that the
wrestlerName property is set correctly for each instance.

To run the test, you'll need to have Node.js installed on your machine.
Open a terminal or command prompt in the directory where the
pick.test.js file is located and run the following command:

```bash
node pick.test.js
```

You should see the output of the test cases in the terminal or command
prompt.

