```

In this example, we have a single test case inside the describe block.
It creates an instance of the Pick class and verifies that the
wrestlerName property is set correctly using the expect assertion. If
the expectation is met, the test will pass.

Feel free to add more test cases to the pick.test.js file to cover
different scenarios or additional properties of the Pick class.

Move switchUser over to user.js and test it. (#13)

https://chat.openai.com/share/e250d511-2739-4533-82aa-141aa01e31ad

Great, both of these files have been added to my codebase and committed
to the main branch. Now tell me how to write the updated game.js and
index.html

ChatGPT
Perfect. Here's the updated Game.js class, which imports and utilizes
the User class:

game.js:

```javascript
import User from './user.js';

export default class Game {
    constructor() {
        this.user = new User();
    }

    startPlaying() {
        const rikishi = document.querySelector('#rikishi').value;
        const picks = this.user.getPicks();
        const message = "You selected: " + rikishi + "\nPrevious Picks: " + JSON.stringify(picks);
        this.user.updatePicks(rikishi);
        return message;
    }

    switchUser() {
        const newUser = document.querySelector('#userSwitch').value;
        this.user.switchUser(newUser);
        document.querySelector('#user').textContent = 'Current user: ' + newUser;
    }

    backfillResults() {
        const contestName = document.querySelector('#backfillContest').value;
        const rikishi = document.querySelector('#backfillRikishi').value;
        this.user.backfillResults(contestName, rikishi);
        this.user.displayBackfilledResults();
    }

    provideFeedback(message) {
        document.querySelector('#feedback').textContent = message;
    }

    initialize() {
        const userElement = document.querySelector('#user');
        if (userElement) {
            userElement.textContent = 'Current user: ' + this.user.name;
        }
        this.user.displayBackfilledResults();

        // Add event listeners
        document.querySelector("#startPlayingButton").addEventListener('click', () => this.startPlaying());
        document.querySelector("#switchUserButton").addEventListener('click', () => this.switchUser());
        document.querySelector("#backfillResultsButton").addEventListener('click', () => this.backfillResults());
    }
}
```

Here's the updated index.html which now imports both Game and User
modules:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Banzuke Surfing Game</title>
    <script src="https://ajax.googleapis.com/ajax/libs/jquery/3.5.1/jquery.min.js"></script>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Welcome to Banzuke Surfing Game!</h1>
    <p id="user"></p>
    <p>Select your Rikishi and start playing!</p>
    <select id="rikishi">
        <option value="1">Rikishi 1</option>
        <option value="2">Rikishi 2</option>
        <!-- more options here -->
    </select>
    <button id="startPlayingButton">Start Playing</button>
    <hr>
    <h2>Backfilled Results:</h2>
    <div id="backfilledResults"></div>
    <h2>Admin Panel</h2>
    <p>Switch user:</p>
    <input id="userSwitch" type="text">
    <button id="switchUserButton">Switch User</button>
    <p>Backfill contest results:</p>
    <input id="backfillContest" type="text" placeholder="Enter contest name">
    <input id="backfillRikishi" type="text" placeholder="Enter rikishi selection">
    <button id="backfillResultsButton">Backfill Results</button>
    <p id="feedback"></p>
    <script type="module">
        import { Game } from './game.js';
        window.game = new Game();
        window.game.initialize();
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

With these changes, the Game class becomes more streamlined and focuses
on game-level behaviors, while the User class takes care of user-related
logic and operations. This structure enhances modularity and separation
of concerns in your code.

User
if my understanding is accurate, then you made no changes to index.html
- is that right?

now i'd like to understand why do I need switchUser in both modules. Why
not let the user set the onclick and game be oblivious?

ChatGPT
You're correct, my apologies for the confusion - there's no need to make
any changes to the index.html file as the changes we made are primarily
at the JavaScript level.

As for the switchUser function, you're also correct in saying that it
can be handled at a more user-focused level. The Game class does not
necessarily need to be aware of the user switch action as it's primarily
related to the User's state. The Game class's primary concern should be
to facilitate game-related actions, and the state of the User (including
switching users) could be managed by the User class itself.

You can indeed set up the event listener for user switching in the User
class, and this can be done when the page is loaded or when a new User
instance is created. Here's how you can do that:

```javascript
export default class User {
    // ...

    initialize() {
        this.displayBackfilledResults();
        document.querySelector("#user").textContent = 'Current user: ' + this.name;

        // Setup event listener for switching users
        document.querySelector("#switchUserButton").addEventListener('click', () => {
            const newUser = document.querySelector('#userSwitch').value;
            this.switchUser(newUser);
            document.querySelector("#user").textContent = 'Current user: ' + newUser;
        });
    }
}
```
And in your Game.js, the initialize method:

```javascript
initialize() {
    this.user.initialize();

    // Add event listeners
    document.querySelector("#startPlayingButton").addEventListener('click', () => this.startPlaying());
    document.querySelector("#backfillResultsButton").addEventListener('click', () => this.backfillResults());
}
```

In this way, the User class takes care of its own state transitions
(including user switching), and the Game class doesn't need to be
concerned with that.

