I apologize for the confusion. To avoid inlining JavaScript code in the HTML file and keep the separation of concerns intact, you can create a new initialization function in the game.js file that sets up the event listeners. Then, you can call this function in the HTML file.

Here is how to do this:

Modify game.js to export a function that sets up the event listeners:
```javascript
class Game {
  //... existing class methods
}

// Export a function to initialize game
function initGame() {
  const game = new Game();

  document.querySelector("#startPlayingButton").addEventListener('click', () => game.startPlaying());
  document.querySelector("#switchUserButton").addEventListener('click', () => game.switchUser());
  document.querySelector("#backfillResultsButton").addEventListener('click', () => game.backfillResults());
}

// Expose initGame function to the global scope
window.initGame = initGame;
```

Call initGame function in index.html:
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Banzuke Surfing Game</title>
    <script src="https://ajax.googleapis.com/ajax/libs/jquery/3.5.1/jquery.min.js"></script>
</head>
<body>
    <!-- The rest of your HTML content -->

    <!-- Link to the external JavaScript file -->
    <script src="game.js"></script>
    <script>
        // Initialize game
        initGame();
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```
This way, the JavaScript code related to the Game class remains in the game.js file, and the HTML file is only responsible for calling the initialization function, preserving the separation of concerns between HTML and JavaScript.

